---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { routePath } from '../../i18n/routes';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const p = (key: Parameters<typeof routePath>[1]) => routePath(locale, key);

const features = [
  { label: 'INDEX', title: 'Crawling and indexing', text: 'Can Google find your pages, and does it index them? We check your service and district pages one by one.' },
  { label: 'SPEED', title: 'Speed on mobile', text: 'People who come from the map open your site on their phone. We make sure the first screen loads fast and doesn’t shift while loading.' },
  { label: 'SCHEMA', title: 'Structured data', text: 'We add your business details to the page in a form Google can read. Name, address and phone match your profile.' },
  { label: 'PROFILE', title: 'Profile link', text: 'Does the website link on your profile go to the right page? In a business with branches, each profile links to its own branch page.' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'nedir', label: 'What does technical SEO do?' },
  { id: 'indeks', label: 'Are your pages on Google?' },
  { id: 'yerel', label: 'What we check on a local business site' },
  { id: 'hiz', label: 'The effect of site speed' },
  { id: 'schema', label: 'Structured data' },
  { id: 'dil', label: 'More than one language' },
  { id: 'sonra', label: 'After the audit' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'My site runs on WordPress. Can you do technical SEO on it?',
    a: 'Yes. On WordPress we see two problems most: pages weighed down by plugins, and unnecessary tag and archive pages getting indexed. Both can be fixed without rebuilding the site.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will structured data raise my rankings?',
    a: 'Not directly. It lets Google understand the page and the business correctly, and opens up rich results in some searches. We set it up mainly to make the link between your profile and your site clear.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is technical SEO done once and finished?',
    a: 'The big fixes are done once. New errors can appear every time the site changes; a theme update or a new plugin is enough. That’s why we repeat the crawl regularly.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need to hand over management of my site to you?',
    a: 'Yes. If we can’t manage the whole site, we don’t start. We make the fixes, and you see what we did in the report and the client portal every week.',
  },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  locale={locale}
  route="services/technical-seo"
  title="Technical SEO Service | Wiserrise"
  description="Can Google crawl your site, and are your pages indexed? We fix indexing, speed, schema and the profile link on local business websites."
  eyebrow="SERVICE · TECHNICAL SEO"
  h1="Open your site properly to Google with technical SEO."
  crumb="Technical SEO"
  lede="Technical SEO is the work we do so Google can crawl your site and index your pages. For a local business, that means your service and district pages work both in search and on the map. We crawl the site first, then close the errors we find in order of impact."
  serviceName="Technical SEO"
  breadcrumb={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'Services', href: p('services') },
    { label: 'SEO', href: p('services/seo') },
  ]}
  features={features}
  toc={toc}
  faq={faq}
  related={[
    { label: 'SEO', href: p('services/seo'), text: 'Groundwork, page structure, content and links: the main service this one belongs to.' },
    { label: 'Local SEO', href: p('services/local-seo'), text: 'We fix your profile and your site together and work on getting you to stand out on the map.' },
    { label: 'On-Page SEO', href: p('services/on-page-seo'), text: 'We fit each page to a single search and fix titles and internal links.' },
    { label: 'Content Production', href: p('services/content'), text: 'We write the service and district pages missing from your site.' },
  ]}
  ctaLabel="Get a free analysis"
  ctaHref="/#contact"
>
  <h2 id="nedir">What does technical SEO do?</h2>

  <p>
    It makes sure your pages reach Google. Google first crawls a page, then decides whether to index it. A page that gets
    stuck at either step won’t appear in search, however well it’s written.
  </p>

  <p>
    For a local business there’s a map side to this as well. Your Google Business Profile links to your site, and Google
    also reads from your site which service you offer in which district. A service page Google can’t see is no use on the
    map either; we explain this link in detail on the <a href={p('services/local-seo')}>local SEO</a> page.
  </p>

  <p>
    That’s why we start every job with a technical audit. We don’t write a single line of content for a site that isn’t
    getting indexed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="indeks">Are your pages really on Google?</h2>

  <p>
    Search Console tells you the fastest. The Pages report shows which URLs are indexed, which were left out and on what
    grounds. We start the diagnosis there, then crawl the site from end to end.
  </p>

  <p>What we look at in the crawl:</p>

  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>
      <strong>robots.txt and noindex.</strong> Sections blocked by mistake, or thank-you and filter pages that shouldn’t be
      in the index. Google can’t see a noindex tag on a page blocked by robots.txt, so the two must not be mixed up.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Canonical.</strong> If the same page opens at more than one URL, with and without www or over both http and
      https, you need to tell Google which one is the main one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sitemap.</strong> Is it up to date, does it contain only the pages that should be indexed, has it been
      submitted to Search Console.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Redirects and broken links.</strong> Redirect chains, internal links that return 404 and lost URLs left over
      from the old site.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>When you rebuild your site, list your redirects first.</strong> If each old URL isn’t tied to its new
      counterpart with a 301, the ranking built up at those URLs is lost. It’s the most common traffic loss we see in
      businesses that change web designers.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="yerel">What do we check on a local business site?</h2>

  <p>
    On top of the general crawl, we check four things separately. All of them concern the link between your profile and
    your site.
  </p>

  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>
      <strong>The website link on your profile.</strong> If it goes to a redirect, an old URL or a page that isn’t indexed,
      we fix it. In a business with branches, each profile links to its own branch page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Name, address and phone.</strong> They’re written on the page as text and match the spelling on the profile
      exactly. The phone number can be called with one tap on a phone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Service and district pages.</strong> Do they get links from the menu or from other pages? Google finds a page
      with no links late, and sometimes never.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The mobile version.</strong> Google evaluates the site by looking at its mobile version. Content that exists
      on desktop and is missing on mobile doesn’t exist for Google.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    This part is the site side of the work. We handle the profile side in
    <a href={p('services/gbp')}>Google Business Profile management</a>, and the two run within the same plan.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hiz">Does site speed affect your rankings?</h2>

  <p>
    It does, though less than people think. The measures Google calls Core Web Vitals look at how fast the main content
    appears (LCP), how fast the page responds to a tap (INP) and how much the page shifts while loading (CLS).
  </p>

  <p>
    Speed matters most for the customer. A person coming from the map has already decided and is about to call you or get directions. If the page opens slowly, they go back and move on to the next business on the list.
  </p>

  <p>
    Our goal is that a person arriving on a phone sees the first screen right away. Most of the work is serving images at
    the right size, deferring scripts that hold the page up, dropping unused CSS and JavaScript and lightening fonts. We
    track the PageSpeed score as a by-product of this work.
  </p>

  <h2 id="schema">What does structured data do?</h2>

  <p>
    It writes your business details onto the page in a form Google reads as a machine. In the shared language called
    Schema.org, you’re saying “this is a dental clinic, this is its address, these are the services it offers”. The types
    we use for local businesses:
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Type</th>
          <th>What it does</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>LocalBusiness</strong> and its subtypes (such as Dentist, Physiotherapy, LegalService)</td>
          <td>Defines the business and matches the profile and the site as the same business</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Service</strong></td>
          <td>Says which service a service page describes and who provides it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>sameAs</strong></td>
          <td>Ties the site, the Google profile and social accounts to the same business</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>BreadcrumbList</strong></td>
          <td>Describes where the page sits on the site</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>FAQPage</strong></td>
          <td>Makes questions and answers readable. Since 2023 Google shows the expandable result only for well-known government and health sites.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    What we pay attention to when setting it up: the schemas on every page are tied to the same business with a fixed @id.
    If you write disconnected schema page by page, Google may take the same business for several separate entities.
  </p>

  <p>
    The name, address and phone in the schema must match your profile. Writing “Mavi Çınar Fizyoterapi” on the profile and
    leaving the old company name in the schema means showing Google two separate businesses.
  </p>

  <h2 id="dil">What if your site is in more than one language?</h2>

  <p>
    You set up hreflang. This tag tells Google “the English version of this page is here, the German version is here” and
    shows the right language to the right searcher.
  </p>

  <p>
    Say you’re a dental clinic in Antalya that also treats patients from abroad, and your site is in three languages. The
    most common mistake we see is every page pointing hreflang at every language, including pages that have no translation.
    Google ignores hreflang signals that have no counterpart or don’t point back.
  </p>

  <p>
    In a correct setup, hreflang connects only pages that really have a translation, each page also lists itself, and an
    x-default is defined. District pages written only for people searching in Turkish stay outside this set.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sonra">What happens after the audit?</h2>

  <p>
    We rank the findings by impact. First we close the errors that keep pages out of the index; speed and schema come after
    them. Trying to fix everything in the same week makes it hard to see what worked.
  </p>

  <p>
    We make the fixes ourselves, because if we can’t manage the whole site, we don’t start. We explain where this rule
    comes from on the <a href={`${p('services/local-seo')}#site`}>local SEO page</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Once the foundation is solid, it’s the pages’ turn. We fit each page to a single search with
    <a href={p('services/on-page-seo')} style="white-space: nowrap;">on-page SEO</a>, and write the missing service and
    district pages as part of <a href={p('services/content')}>content production</a>.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="cta">
    <h2>Are your pages really getting <span class="highlight">indexed</span>?</h2>
    <p>
      In the free analysis we look at your site and your profile and list the problems to fix first, in order. The result
      lands in your inbox within <span style="white-space: nowrap;">24 hours</span>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ContentLayout>
